```python
def solve(board):
    cell = find_empty(board)
    if cell is None:
        return True                 # every cell filled — solved
    row, col = cell
    for digit in '123456789':
        if is_legal(board, row, col, digit):
            board[row][col] = digit
            if solve(board):
                return True
            board[row][col] = '.'   # undo — this digit didn't lead to a solution
    return False                    # no digit worked here — this whole branch is dead
```

The base case is "no empty cell left," meaning the current assignment is a complete solution. `is_legal` is where the row/column/box constraints get checked — naively a scan, ideally a set lookup (next slide) — and undoing a failed digit before trying the next one is the same unexplore step from every other backtracking problem in this group.
